1
00:00:00,725 --> 00:00:03,535
Thirteen metres from home, carrying 21 samples,

2
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:05,660
this rover's battery runs flat.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:08,260
Its autopilot was written by Claude Opus.

4
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:10,475
Score for this site: zero.

5
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:15,548
This is Lightlag Traverse, a reinforcement learning environment.

6
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:18,435
An agent drives a rover across Mars-like ground,

7
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:20,935
and every command lands three ticks late.

8
00:00:20,975 --> 00:00:23,973
The sun charges the battery; driving drains it.

9
00:00:24,013 --> 00:00:27,997
More targets than a day allows, three sites in a row, no reset.

10
00:00:28,037 --> 00:00:29,900
Every rule is public.

11
00:00:30,625 --> 00:00:32,285
Claude doesn't fail at coding.

12
00:00:32,325 --> 00:00:34,860
Within minutes, it writes a real autopilot.

13
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,000
It fails at planning a whole day: the route, the battery, the way home.

14
00:00:39,550 --> 00:00:43,375
Here, at 9% battery, our reference waits for the sun.

15
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:47,422
Four ticks later, Claude's rover drives its battery to zero.

16
00:00:47,462 --> 00:00:49,850
Waiting six ticks would have brought it home.

17
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:54,568
Claude's own report: the planner only counted time.

18
00:00:54,608 --> 00:00:57,218
Its program played all three sites in under a minute,

19
00:00:57,258 --> 00:00:59,733
before Claude checked a single result.

20
00:01:00,858 --> 00:01:03,418
The reference is a planner, not a model.

21
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:05,330
It tracks the rover through the delay,

22
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:07,168
learns how each terrain slips,

23
00:01:07,208 --> 00:01:09,918
and prices every leg of its route against the sun.

24
00:01:09,958 --> 00:01:12,458
It is always home before the day ends.

25
00:01:13,683 --> 00:01:17,593
Through Harbor and OpenCode, the reference scores 0.48.

26
00:01:17,633 --> 00:01:20,743
Seven Claude runs, across Opus, Sonnet and Haiku,

27
00:01:20,783 --> 00:01:22,993
all score below 0.3.

28
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:24,305
Three lost a rover.

29
00:01:24,345 --> 00:01:26,258
Doing nothing scores zero.

30
00:01:27,458 --> 00:01:28,368
Was that luck?

31
00:01:28,408 --> 00:01:32,793
On Modal, we replayed six of Claude's programs on 300 fresh worlds.

32
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:37,408
The reference won at least 288 times, and never lost a rover.

33
00:01:38,683 --> 00:01:46,205
Also on Modal, a small policy trained from scratch improves from 0.09 to 0.38 in development,

34
00:01:46,245 --> 00:01:49,458
still short of a greedy controller and the reference.

35
00:01:50,683 --> 00:01:52,318
One command reruns it all,

36
00:01:52,358 --> 00:01:55,318
and a live demo on Modal plays any world you pick.

37
00:01:55,358 --> 00:01:57,083
Can your agent make it home?

